{
  "concept": "Training a model means moving a point through parameter space: every (intercept φ₀, slope φ₁) pair is one location on the loss surface and one line through the data, and gradient descent walks downhill on the surface until the line fits.",
  "figureType": "other",
  "elements": [
    "Two side-by-side panels with labels 'a)' (left) and 'b)' (right)",
    "Panel a title: 'Loss, L[φ]'",
    "Panel a: filled contour heatmap of the least-squares loss over parameter space, dark red-brown at the basin, lightening to pale cream toward the upper-left and right edges, with thin light-grey iso-loss contour rings forming a tilted elliptical bowl",
    "Panel a x-axis: 'Intercept, φ₀', ticks at 0.0, 1.0, 2.0",
    "Panel a y-axis: 'Slope, φ₁', ticks at -1.0 (top), 0.0, 1.0 (bottom) — axis is inverted, slope increases downward",
    "Panel a: gradient-descent path — 5 circular markers numbered 0,1,2,3,4 connected by a thick grey polyline with a small arrowhead into marker 4",
    "Panel a: marker colors ramp with iteration from pale teal #b4e1dd (0) through #8bd0c9, #4db7aa to dark teal #2f7870 (4); white numeric labels beside each marker",
    "Panel a: draggable current-parameter marker (highlighted ring) sitting on the path",
    "Panel b x-axis: 'Input, x', ticks 0.0, 1.0, 2.0",
    "Panel b y-axis: 'Output, y', ticks 0.0, 1.0, 2.0",
    "Panel b: ~12 salmon/terracotta (#c8674a) scatter points of training data lying along a rising trend from about (0.05, 0.75) to (1.9, 1.6)",
    "Panel b: five straight fitted lines, one per iteration, colored with the same pale-to-dark teal ramp; line 0 nearly flat and low, each successive line steeper/higher, line 4 (dark teal) passing through the data cloud",
    "Panel b: small teal numerals 0,1,2,3,4 labelling each line near the left edge, with a curved grey arrow sweeping from label 0 up to label 4 showing the progression",
    "Panel b: highlighted 'current' fitted line corresponding to the draggable marker",
    "Optional residual segments from each data point to the current line",
    "Loss readout annotation showing L[φ] for the current parameter pair"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "dragParams",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "That one point in parameter space IS one line in data space — dragging the marker anywhere on the loss surface instantly redraws the fitted line, and the darker the region, the worse the fit."
    },
    {
      "id": "intercept",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Intercept, φ₀",
      "range": [0, 2, 0.01],
      "default": 0.12,
      "teaches": "Changing φ₀ alone slides the marker horizontally across the loss contours while the fitted line shifts up or down without changing its tilt."
    },
    {
      "id": "slope",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Slope, φ₁",
      "range": [-1, 1, 0.01],
      "default": -0.13,
      "teaches": "Changing φ₁ alone slides the marker vertically across the contours while the fitted line pivots, showing that the two parameters move the point along independent axes of the same surface."
    },
    {
      "id": "descentStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Gradient descent step",
      "states": ["0", "1", "2", "3", "4"],
      "default": "0",
      "teaches": "That optimization is a sequence of downhill steps: each click moves the marker toward the basin and visibly tightens the line against the data."
    },
    {
      "id": "showResiduals",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show residuals",
      "options": ["hidden", "shown"],
      "default": "hidden",
      "teaches": "That the height of the loss surface is literally the sum of squared vertical gaps drawn in the data panel."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A bad guess",
      "narration": "We start at point 0: a small intercept and a slightly negative slope. On the loss surface the marker sits in a dark, high-loss region; in the data panel the corresponding line is flat and far below the points.",
      "state": { "descentStep": "0", "intercept": 0.12, "slope": -0.13, "showResiduals": "hidden" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Loss = summed squared errors",
      "narration": "Turn on the residuals. Each grey stub is one data point's error; the height of the loss surface at the marker is just the sum of their squares.",
      "state": { "descentStep": "0", "showResiduals": "shown" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the intercept only",
      "narration": "Push φ₀ to the right. The marker travels horizontally across the contours and the line rises bodily without tilting — the loss drops, but only partway.",
      "state": { "intercept": 0.85, "slope": -0.13, "showResiduals": "shown" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the slope only",
      "narration": "Now increase φ₁. The marker drops vertically into the light-centred basin and the line rotates to match the trend of the data.",
      "state": { "intercept": 0.85, "slope": 0.5, "showResiduals": "shown" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Let gradient descent do it",
      "narration": "Step the optimizer. Each click takes one downhill step from 0 to 4; the steps shorten as the surface flattens near the minimum, and the fitted lines fan up toward the data.",
      "state": { "descentStep": "4", "showResiduals": "hidden" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Explore off the path",
      "narration": "Drag the marker anywhere on the surface. Every location you can reach is a legal model — the whole job of training is finding the one at the bottom of the bowl.",
      "state": { "descentStep": "4", "showResiduals": "shown" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A point on the loss surface and a line through the data are the same object seen twice — descending the surface and fitting the data are literally one motion.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two equal-size square panels side by side. Left panel 'a)': a tilted elliptical loss bowl rendered as a smooth heatmap, darkest (deep red-brown) at the basin running diagonally from lower-left to upper-right, lightening to cream in the far upper-left corner and along the right edge; overlay ~15 thin light-grey iso-loss contours. x-axis 'Intercept, φ₀' 0→2, y-axis 'Slope, φ₁' labelled -1.0 at the TOP and 1.0 at the BOTTOM (inverted axis). Draw the descent path as a thick grey polyline over 5 filled circles labelled 0–4 in white, starting upper-left around (0.12, -0.13) and curving down-right to about (0.85, 0.50), with steps clearly shrinking and a small arrowhead entering marker 4; marker fill ramps pale teal #b4e1dd → #8bd0c9 → #4db7aa → #2f7870. Right panel 'b)': white background, axes 'Input, x' and 'Output, y' both 0→2. Scatter ~12 terracotta (#c8674a) dots with white edges rising roughly linearly with mild noise from near (0.05,0.75) to (1.9,1.6). Five straight fitted lines in the same teal ramp, all clipped to the axes: line 0 nearly horizontal and low, lines 1–3 progressively steeper and higher, line 4 the darkest and best-fitting; number each line 0–4 in matching teal near the left edge and add a curved grey arrow sweeping from 0 up to 4. Highlight the currently selected parameter marker and its line with extra weight. Keep the two panels visually linked: any change in parameter space must animate the corresponding line in data space in the same frame."
}

